Hey, <@!211091239112671234> and <@!683653554631868440>,

I heard you were discussing JUMPDEST analysis issues.

evmone/Baseline is using different bitmap than geth. I only keep 1 bits for valid JUMPDEST locations.
I spent some time on optimizing it recently. There is some progress, but not 100% sure of results because on my laptop there seems to be a regression with one of the compilers.
Here are the results: https://github.com/ethereum/evmone/pull/306

There is also a more fancy implementation, I will benchmark it some other time.
```cpp
    for (size_t i = 0; i < code_size; ++i)
    {
        const auto op = code[i];
        const auto data_len = op - size_t{OP_PUSH1 - 1};
        if (data_len <= 32)
            i += data_len;
        else if (INTX_UNLIKELY(op == OP_JUMPDEST))
            m[i] = true;
    }
```

Do you have information how fast is the geth analysis? For me the lower bound seems to be ~700MB/s.

Finally, I tried to accelerate it with SIMD without success. I can share some code, but I'm SIMD noob. Maybe some SIMD hacker can solve this. There is generic problem description for that: https://notes.ethereum.org/@chfast/fast-evm-bytecode-analysis.
